---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import Endmark from "../components/Endmark.astro";

const socials = [
  { href: "mailto:sundharesansk11@gmail.com", label: "Email" },
  { href: "https://x.com/sundharesansk11", label: "X" },
  { href: "https://www.linkedin.com/in/sundharesan-kumaresan-iyal11", label: "LinkedIn" },
  { href: "https://github.com/sundharesan11", label: "GitHub" },
  { href: "https://medium.com/@sundharesansk11", label: "Medium" },
  { href: "https://www.instagram.com/sundharesan_11/", label: "Instagram" },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About · Sundharesan Kumaresan"
  description="The long version: a forward-deployed engineer, a writer, and one stubborn question about who a system gets to see."
  showFooter={false}
>
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto max-w-page px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <PageHeader left="The long version" right="Forward Deployed Engineer · Oogway Labs" />
    <section data-reveal class="mt-8 grid gap-10 md:min-h-[calc(100vh-8rem)] md:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] md:items-start md:gap-14">
      <div class="md:sticky md:top-32">
        <h1 data-reveal class="reveal-tracking font-serif text-display-read font-semibold leading-[0.95]">Sundharesan Kumaresan</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-[42ch] text-lg text-text-muted">I build close to the problem, write around the strange parts, and keep returning to one question: <span class="text-text">who a system notices, and who it never has to.</span></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="aspect-[2/3] min-h-[420px] w-full max-w-[460px] overflow-hidden bg-bg md:sticky md:top-32 md:order-first md:min-h-[min(72vh,720px)] md:max-w-[520px]">
        <img
          src="/img/about.jpg"
          width="2656"
          height="3984"
          alt="Portrait of Sundharesan Kumaresan"
          class="h-full w-full object-cover object-center"
        />
      </figure>
    </section>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-16"><Prose dropcap>
      <p>Most people meet me through a problem: something half-defined, slightly impossible, and already late. I like the part where it turns into a real thing someone can use. The surprise, at least to people who prefer tidy bios, is that I write too.</p>
      <p>Not as a second lane. More like the same itch with different tools. Code makes a system legible enough to use. Stories make a person legible enough to understand. I keep returning to both because apparently one complicated thing at a time was not enough.</p>
      <p>At Oogway Labs, I&rsquo;m a forward-deployed engineer. The title is a grand way of saying I stay close to the problem: hear what people are trying to do, shape the product around what would actually help, then build it. Some days that means architecture and product judgment. Some days it means making the thing less clever and more useful, which is rude of reality, but usually correct.</p>
      <p>I&rsquo;m learning AI where it has to behave, inside products people rely on, with legacy software, strange edge cases, and human patience all involved. I trust a new model exactly as far as I have personally tried to break it. The job is part LLM craft, part old-school engineering, part listening carefully enough that the next build has a reason to exist.</p>
      <p>Lately I&rsquo;ve been reading my way into psychology too. Every product that works is sitting on top of how people think, and pretending otherwise gets you software no one uses. The less professional reason is simpler: people are the strangest, most interesting thing I have ever tried to understand. I&rsquo;d like to be a little less wrong about us by the end of it.</p>
      <p>I do not write fiction as often as I would like. Most of the time I am carrying around an overthought observation that is not yet interesting enough to deserve daylight. Give it a character, a room, a bad decision, and sometimes it becomes worth looking at.</p>
      <p>Fiction is where I get to think sideways. Economics and technical writing ask me to be clear and useful. Stories let me be more expressive, follow the strange parts for a while, and see whether the thing I have been quietly overthinking can turn into something another person might recognise. When it works, it reveals more than I meant to say. Which is inconvenient, but probably the point.</p>
      <p>Underneath all of it sits one question I cannot put down: who a system is built to count, and who it quietly does not. It is not abstract. The people who grow our food, the kid in a small town who could medal for the country, the worker who knows where everything actually breaks, they are often invisible to the systems that could change their lives. That invisibility is a choice someone made, usually by accident, always with consequences.</p>
      <p>That question is also where I am headed. I have not built every answer yet, and I am not going to pretend otherwise. Right now I am doing the unglamorous part: learning the economics and logistics, building close to real users, and testing the ideas that need to survive contact with the world. The ambitions live under To. The active research lives in <a href="/iyal" class="font-script text-3xl leading-none text-accent transition-colors hover:text-accent-hover">iyal</a>. Both are still earning the right to become more.</p>
      <p>There is also modelling. No shoots, no catalogue, no agent sending a PDF called “Sundharesan Selects”. There are a few decent shots somewhere, but I have kept them off the internet. The urge has been hanging around for years, annoyingly persistent, so it earned its own page. Maybe it becomes something. Maybe I discover I am not nearly as photogenic before coffee as I had hoped. Either way, I am giving it a proper chance to take shape.<Endmark /></p>
    </Prose></div>
    <blockquote data-reveal class="reveal-scale mt-24 max-w-[20ch] border-l-2 border-stellar pl-8 font-serif text-display-read font-semibold leading-[1.02]">
      Who a system sees is a moral choice.
    </blockquote>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-16">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-kicker text-text-muted">Elsewhere, same person</p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm">
        {socials.map((s) => (
          <a href={s.href} class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">{s.label}</a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
